# 166 · Agent definitions you can read and change: one definition for each role in code, and the prompt each run gives it

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011) · **Depends on:** [160](_completed/160-v010-audit.goal)

## What

Every agent role Thermite runs has one definition in code, in one folder, that a person can read and change: the workers, the research workers, the planners, the auditors, the teachers and the review panel, and every other role that sends a prompt to a model.

1. **One definition for each role.** It holds the role's instructions, its tools, the shape of its answer, which model slot and agent program it uses, and its limits. Text that several roles share is written once.
2. **How a run changes it.** One function for each role assembles what a given run adds: the brief, the contract, the campaign, the kit, directives, rules, field notes, past research, the queue and the owner's messages. It can render the exact prompt for any run, without calling a model.
3. **Shown everywhere.** The dashboard, REST, MCP and the command line show each role's definition, and the prompt a chosen run gives it (for example `thermite agents show <role> --run <run>`). A docs page lists the roles and what each one does.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-26: "Agent definitions. I want to be able to see these in code and modify them (workers, researchers, auditors, teachers, etc) and how they are modified for a given brief etc..."

## Constraints

- **Agents propose; code judges.** The definitions live in code the agents cannot write. A role's text never decides a measurement, a verdict, completion or promotion.
- **No default caps.** A definition holds only the limits that exist today; it adds none.
- **One fact, one home.** A definition is the only place a role's text or tool list is kept; the adapters for Pi, OpenCode, Claude Code and Codex read it.
- **Docs follow the code,** in the same commit.

## Done when

- [ ] Every role has one definition in one folder, and every prompt Thermite sends comes from one of them. A test fails when a prompt is built outside a definition.
- [ ] Each role's assembly for a run is one function, and a golden test holds the rendered prompt of each role for a sample run.
- [ ] The dashboard, REST, MCP and the command line show each definition and a run's rendered prompt.
- [ ] A docs page lists the roles, and the reference pages name the new routes, tools and command.
- [ ] The fast checks, the docs build and check, and the agents, steering, Claude Code and Codex acceptance checks pass.

## What the review found

The audit's lane 9 (`.internal/reviews/v0.1.1-audit/09-agent-definitions.md`) mapped every role on 2026-10-26.

- **No role has one definition.** Thermite runs 15 roles. Their text is spread over about 35 files, and prompts are sent from 37 places in 13 files. Part of the code worker's instructions is in the scheduler's brief builder, not with the role.
- **The prompt a run sent is not kept,** apart from the conversation kept after a compaction, and nothing shows a role's text: no page, route, tool, command or docs page.
- **Variants are made by editing text.** The Claude Code and Codex prompts are OpenCode's, changed with `.replace`.
- **Each answer and tool schema is written two or three times** (zod, TypeBox, the prompt's example).
- **A prompt can't be previewed:** building one also reads the ledger by hand and has side effects (it marks the owner's messages as given and writes `BRIEF.md`).
- **Names:** the code worker calls itself a research worker; the final writer's calls are recorded as the teachers'; the synthesizer and the final writer have no model slot of their own.
- **A bug,** in goal [171](_completed/171-bugs-on-the-wire.goal): OpenCode and Claude Code get the whole prompt as one command-line argument.

## Design (proposed by the review)

```
src/agents/            the shared part (decisions.md, 2026-10-26)
  prompt.ts            Prompt, Section, Source; the exact text sent to a program
  roles/               one module for each role, and shared.ts for text several roles share
  tools/               one table of tools, one zod schema each
  programs/            the table of agent programs (the guide's theme T7)
src/harness/prompts/   what a run adds, read from the ledger, with no side effects
```

- Each role has a pure `render(input)` that returns its prompt as sections, each labelled with its source: the role, the contract, an owner's message, a queue item, field notes. So the owner sees what each brief added.
- Every prompt sent is recorded by code, kept out of the agents' research folder.
- The dashboard, REST, MCP, `thermite agents show <role> --run <run>` and a generated docs page show a role and the prompts a run sent.
- The steps, in goal 125's order: the role names (T1); golden tests of every role's prompt today; the program table, with every prompt sent by standard input; one table of tools; the roles moved byte for byte, then fixed one commit at a time; the record of each prompt; the surfaces; per-campaign additions last.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-26: the audit's lane 9 maps every role and proposes the shape above.
- 2026-10-26, owner: every recommendation of the review is taken:

  1. **May the owner add text to a role for one campaign, without a code change?** Recommended: yes, as additions only, in the contract (signed, versioned, labelled in the prompt), per role; not per kit, and never replacing the role's text. Built last.
  2. **TypeScript modules or Markdown files for the role texts?** Recommended: TypeScript modules, with the prose at the top of each, and a generated docs page that shows it as prose. No build step, and typed names and limits in the text.
  3. **Keep the whole text of every prompt sent?** Recommended: yes, compressed, in the harness's state and the run's exports, never in the agents' research folder; control gets an event with the sections and their sources. Measure the space at 1,000 trials first.
  4. **Model slots of their own for the synthesizer and the final writer?** Recommended: yes, each defaulting to the first teacher's model, with their calls recorded under their own names.
  5. **A preview of a role's next prompt, from control?** Recommended: not in the first pass. Show the prompts sent; serve previews on the rig's own API; add control's preview with the request and reply of goal [168](168-message-bus.goal).
  6. **How the programs receive the role's text.** Recommended: decided in goal [169](169-agents-in-their-terminal.goal); the definitions render a prompt that does not depend on delivery, and until then it goes by standard input or a file, never one argument.
  7. **Should an agent program read the project's own instruction files (such as `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` in the workspace)?** Recommended: not by default; turned off where a program allows it. If a project's instructions are wanted, they are rendered as a labelled section, so they show in the record and every program gets the same prompt.
  8. **Cut a worker's or a planner's first message to fit the model's window?** Recommended: yes, with the advisors' rule: fit the window at the compaction point less 30%, keep the owner's messages and the role's own text whole, and say what was left out and where to read it. It is a mechanical limit on one payload, not a cap on research.

